WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.757
Permissions decide who can do what in an Orchard Core site.

00:00:04.757 --> 00:00:09.150
In this video, you declare permissions in your own module, see how the

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:13.890
roles editor shows them, and check them in a controller, a view, and a menu.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:22.762
A permission is a name that stands for one right, such as ManageWidgets.

00:00:22.762 --> 00:00:26.542
A role holds a set of permission names, and a user receives the

00:00:26.542 --> 00:00:29.390
permissions of every role the user belongs to.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.828
Some roles apply without an assignment.

00:00:32.828 --> 00:00:39.398
The Anonymous role applies to every visitor, and the Authenticated role applies to every signed-in user.

00:00:39.398 --> 00:00:43.340
Users in the Administrator role pass every permission check.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:45.841
Your module has three jobs.

00:00:45.841 --> 00:00:50.882
Declare the permission, provide it to Orchard Core, and check it where the code needs it.

00:00:50.882 --> 00:00:53.110
The next chapters follow that order.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:01.650
Declare each permission once, as a static field of a static class.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:08.160
A Permission takes a name and a description, and the description comes from LocalizationSource.Create.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.827
The name must be unique across every module, ignoring case.

00:01:12.827 --> 00:01:15.660
Roles store that name, so keep it stable.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.999
A duplicate is logged as an error at startup, and the roles editor

00:01:19.999 --> 00:01:22.590
shows Duplicate Permissions Detected.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:29.297
Passing the declaring type, here MyPermissions, gives the description a translation context.

00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:33.796
The roles editor translates it with PO files, using the full name of

00:01:33.796 --> 00:01:35.680
that type as the context.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:42.228
A permission can be security critical, for a right that lets a user elevate other permissions.

00:01:42.228 --> 00:01:46.750
The roles editor marks such a permission with a Security Critical badge.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.536
A permission can also be implied by others.

00:01:50.536 --> 00:01:57.290
ViewWidgets lists ManageWidgets, so a user who holds ManageWidgets also passes a check for ViewWidgets.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.792
To group permissions under a heading of your own, set Category.

00:02:01.792 --> 00:02:06.850
Without it, the roles editor uses the name of the feature that provides the permission.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.828
A class that implements IPermissionProvider lists the permissions of the module.

00:02:15.828 --> 00:02:20.050
GetPermissionsAsync returns every permission that the module declares.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:25.865
GetDefaultStereotypes names roles and the permissions each one receives by default.

00:02:25.865 --> 00:02:29.791
A role with one of these names is granted them when the feature

00:02:29.791 --> 00:02:32.750
is first enabled, or when the role is created.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.774
Stereotypes only add permissions to a role and never remove any.

00:02:37.774 --> 00:02:42.903
Built-in modules usually list the Administrator role too, but users in it pass

00:02:42.903 --> 00:02:45.040
every check without an entry.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:49.337
Register the provider in Startup with AddPermissionProvider.

00:02:49.337 --> 00:02:54.013
When the module's feature is enabled, the roles editor lists its permissions, and

00:02:54.013 --> 00:02:55.410
roles can hold them.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.837
A recipe can grant the permission by name too.

00:02:58.837 --> 00:03:03.008
In the Roles step, the default behavior replaces the permissions of a role,

00:03:03.008 --> 00:03:05.531
so use Add to keep the ones it already has.

00:03:05.531 --> 00:03:08.260
The step also sets the description of the role.

00:03:12.493 --> 00:03:18.110
The roles editor, under Access Control, Roles, lists the permissions of every enabled feature.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:20.020
Let's open the Manager role.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:24.513
The Roles module declares its own permissions with the code you have seen.

00:03:24.996 --> 00:03:30.137
Permissions are grouped by the feature that provides them, shown by name and identifier.

00:03:30.137 --> 00:03:35.606
Each row shows the description from the code, and the Search permissions box filters the rows.

00:03:36.075 --> 00:03:39.147
Manage Roles carries the Security Critical badge.

00:03:39.147 --> 00:03:42.385
This role is allowed it, so the Allow box is ticked.

00:03:42.837 --> 00:03:47.911
View Roles is not ticked under Allow, but it is Effective, because Manage Roles implies it.

00:03:47.911 --> 00:03:50.997
That is the ImpliedBy list of the permission at work.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.376
The Administrator role has no boxes to tick.

00:03:54.376 --> 00:04:00.930
Its editor says that this system role provides comprehensive access by granting all available permissions.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:11.790
In a controller, inject IAuthorizationService and call AuthorizeAsync with the user and the permission.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.090
When the check fails, the action returns Forbid.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.685
The check succeeds when a role that applies to the user holds the

00:04:19.685 --> 00:04:22.392
permission, or a permission that implies it.

00:04:22.392 --> 00:04:25.750
For users in the Administrator role, it always succeeds.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.068
A view injects the same service to decide what to show.

00:04:30.068 --> 00:04:35.274
The Roles module hides its Add Role button from users who do not hold Manage Roles.

00:04:35.274 --> 00:04:38.550
Its controller checks permissions in every action.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.912
A menu item takes a permission through the Permission method.

00:04:42.912 --> 00:04:47.009
The navigation removes the item, and its child items, for users who do

00:04:47.009 --> 00:04:48.680
not hold that permission.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.667
You declare a permission with a name and a description, provide it through

00:04:54.667 --> 00:04:59.054
IPermissionProvider, see it in the roles editor, and check it with AuthorizeAsync.

00:04:59.054 --> 00:05:00.350
Thanks for watching.

